  "expected_results": [
    "The PID sees a failed-low pressure signal and drives the supply-fan VFD toward maximum speed",
    "The true physical duct pressure crosses 4.0 in. H2O without an automatic trip because that safety is deliberately bypassed",
    "Above the simulator's representative 5.0 in. H2O training duct-class limit, the duct structural-failure alarm latches and the AHU graphic shows the ruptured duct state",
    "The ruptured duct relieves downstream pressure while the uncontrolled PID continues demanding fan speed"
  ],
  "completion_criteria": [
    "Student identifies both failed protections: the bad control sensor and the bypassed independent safety",
    "Student explains why the manual-reset high-static switch must be independent of the PID sensor and controller"
  ],
  "instructor_notes": "The 5.0 in. H2O value is the simulator's representative training duct pressure-class limit, not a universal rupture pressure for every real duct system. Use Restart after the exercise to repair the latched damage and clear all forces/faults.",
  "student_objectives": [
    "Distinguish a control sensor from an independent hardwired safety",
    "Observe how closed terminal dampers, a failed-low sensor, and a bypassed safety can combine into a catastrophic fault"
  ]
